    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 1 (Questions 1&ndash;6)</div>\n<p>Few theoretical interventions in twentieth-century political philosophy have exerted as durable an influence as Benedict Anderson's 1983 formulation of the nation as an &ldquo;imagined community.&rdquo; Prior to Anderson's intervention, political theorists generally treated nations either as primordial biological entities rooted in immemorial antiquity or as cynical ideological artifices fabricated by ruling elites to preserve class hegemony. Anderson rejected both paradigms, proposing instead a cultural definition: the nation is an imagined political community that is inherently limited and sovereign. It is &ldquo;imagined&rdquo; not because it is fraudulent, but because the members of even the smallest nation will never personally know, meet, or hear most of their fellow citizens, yet in the minds of each lives the mental image of their profound communion. It is &ldquo;limited&rdquo; because it possesses finite, if elastic, boundaries beyond which lie other nations; no nation aspires to encompass all of humanity. It is &ldquo;sovereign&rdquo; because the concept was born in an era when the Enlightenment and the French Revolution were shattering the divine legitimacy of dynastic monarchy. Finally, it is imagined as a &ldquo;community&rdquo; because, regardless of prevailing inequalities and structural exploitation, the nation is conceived as a horizontal comradeship, a fraternal bond that has enabled millions of ordinary people over the past two centuries not merely to kill, but willingly to die on its behalf.</p>\n<p>The foundational socio-historical condition that facilitated this collective reimagining was what Anderson termed &ldquo;print-capitalism.&rdquo; The convergence of the mechanical printing press with capitalist market expansion in early modern Europe catalyzed the collapse of sacred linguistic hierarchies. Latin, the sacred scriptural language of Western Christendom, had once unified an elite scholastic universe while severing vernacular-speaking majorities from political authority. By publishing vernacular texts to maximize profitability, commercial printers standardized dialects, fostering a new consciousness among disparate populations who could suddenly read the same vernacular idiom. Concurrently, the mass circulation of the daily newspaper and the realistic novel produced a novel conception of temporality: &ldquo;homogeneous, empty time.&rdquo; In medieval theology, events were understood through messianic simultaneity, a divine providential plan wherein past, present, and future converged. In modern print culture, however, citizens began to conceptualize society as a collective entity moving steadily, calendrically forward through time. Reading the morning newspaper transformed into an extraordinary mass ritual, a secular ceremony performed in silent solitude by millions of strangers simultaneously, reaffirming their shared national trajectory.</p>\n<p>Despite its profound analytical resonance, Anderson's framework has provoked searching critiques from post-colonial theorists, most notably Partha Chatterjee. Chatterjee argued that Anderson's paradigm relies on a Eurocentric, modular conception of nationalism. According to Anderson, the models of national identity were initially pioneered in the Americas and Western Europe and subsequently exported to the colonized world, where anti-colonial movements supposedly &ldquo;chose&rdquo; from ready-made European templates. Chatterjee contended that this modular framework deprives post-colonial societies of genuine historical agency. In his study of Indian anti-colonial nationalism, Chatterjee demonstrated that the colonized did not merely copy Western political blueprints. Decades before launching direct political confrontations against the British colonial state, anti-colonial intellectuals had already asserted sovereign autonomy within an inner, spiritual domain of culture, language, literature, and domestic life, deliberately insulating these realms from Western colonial influence. By treating political statecraft as the sole arena of nation-making, Anderson overlooked the culturally autonomous, contested spaces where non-Western communities imagined their sovereignty long before achieving formal statehood.</p>",
