    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 2 (Questions 7&ndash;12)</div>\n<p>For more than a century, archaeological epistemology was dominated by a naive empiricist assumption: that material artifacts function as mute, objective mirrors of past human behavior. In the mid-twentieth century, the emergence of processual archaeology&mdash;spearheaded by Lewis Binford under the banner of the &ldquo;New Archaeology&rdquo;&mdash;sought to convert the discipline into a rigorous, hypothesis-testing natural science. Processualists conceptualized human culture as an extrasomatic means of adaptation, a systematic behavioral matrix through which human populations adapted to their ecological environments. From this perspective, an excavated potsherd, stone blade, or faunal bone was an unmediated data point reflecting functional utility, subsistence strategies, and ecological pressures. The processualist believed that by applying quantitative modeling, radiocarbon dating, and spatial statistics, the archaeologist could reconstruct past social systems with objective certainty, sweeping aside the subjective impressions of traditional culture-historical narrative.</p>\n<p>Beginning in the 1980s, however, post-processual archaeology mounted a devastating epistemological critique against this scientific reductionism. Led by Ian Hodder and inspired by hermeneutics, structuralism, and critical theory, post-processualists argued that artifacts do not possess fixed, objective meanings waiting to be passively uncovered. Rather, material culture is inherently &ldquo;polysemic&rdquo;&mdash;infused with multiple, shifting symbolic meanings that are actively manipulated by historical actors. An earthenware vessel is never merely a thermodynamic device for boiling water; it is a semiotic medium laden with ideological codes, signaling gender roles, lineage prestige, or ritual taboos. Furthermore, post-processualists insisted that artifacts are not inert passive residues of human action; they are active agents that shape human perception, structure social relationships, and reinforce or contest asymmetries of power. To treat artifacts strictly as ecological adaptations is to strip past humans of intentionality, symbolic imagination, and political agency, reducing culture to a passive thermodynamic mechanism.</p>\n<p>This epistemological rebellion fundamentally destabilized the relationship between material culture and written historical texts. For generations, traditional historians relegated archaeology to the status of an &ldquo;ancillary handmaiden&rdquo; to history, useful only for illustrating narratives already established by documentary records. Yet post-processualists pointed out that written texts are themselves material artifacts, produced overwhelmingly by literate, male, socio-economic elites with vested interests in legitimizing their authority. Material culture, by contrast, frequently bears witness to the lived realities of marginalized populations&mdash;enslaved laborers, domestic women, illiterate subalterns&mdash;whose voices were systematically excluded from formal archival registries. An excavated garbage midden or domestic dwelling often contradicts the triumphalist propaganda of monumental royal inscriptions, revealing covert patterns of cultural resistance and daily negotiation that textual historiography obscures.</p>\n<p>Recently, archaeological theory has moved toward a sophisticated synthesis known as &ldquo;symmetrical archaeology&rdquo; or &ldquo;material engagement theory.&rdquo; Proposed by theorists such as Colin Renfrew and Lambros Malafouris, this framework avoids both processual ecological determinism and radical post-processual textualism. Instead of viewing artifacts either as biological adaptations or as arbitrary linguistic symbols, material engagement theory conceptualizes human cognition as fundamentally extended: mind and matter co-constitute one another through physical practice. When a potter shapes clay, the human mind does not simply project an internal blueprint onto dead matter; the physical affordances and resistance of the clay actively guide the neural and cognitive processes of the artisan. Humans make things, but things inevitably make humans.</p>",
