    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 3 (Questions 13&ndash;18)</div>\n<p>In his bestselling 2005 book <i>Collapse: How Societies Choose to Fail or Succeed</i>, geographer Jared Diamond popularized a compelling cautionary tale of ecological disaster. Diamond argued that throughout human history, numerous grand civilizations&mdash;most notably the Polynesian society of Easter Island (Rapa Nui), the ancestral Puebloans of Chaco Canyon, and the Norse colonies of southwestern Greenland&mdash;succumbed to self-inflicted environmental degradation, or &ldquo;ecocide.&rdquo; According to Diamond's narrative, past societies inadvertently depleted their fragile natural resource bases through deforestation, overhunting, topsoil erosion, and population overshoot. Trapped in rigid ideological frameworks and blinded by short-term elite competition, these cultures failed to adapt to impending resource thresholds. When environmental carrying capacities collapsed, catastrophic societal disintegration ensued, marked by endemic warfare, mass starvation, and demographic decimation. Diamond framed these historical case studies as urgent allegories for modern industrial society, warning that our contemporary failure to manage climate change and ecological limits risks reproducing the tragic fate of Rapa Nui.</p>\n<p>While Diamond's apocalyptic parables captured public imagination, they provoked intense repudiation from anthropologists, archaeologists, and indigenous scholars, culminating in the 2009 rebuttal volume <i>Questioning Collapse</i>, edited by Patricia McAnany and Norman Yoffee. Critics argued that Diamond's thesis relies on outdated colonial stereotypes, selective evidence, and a heavy dose of environmental determinism. The critique was particularly devastating regarding Diamond's flagship case study: Easter Island. In Diamond's telling, Rapa Nui islanders obsessively felled every tree on the island to transport colossal stone statues (moai) in an egotistical display of aristocratic vanity, triggering total ecological collapse, civil war, and cannibalism before European contact. However, rigorous field research led by archaeologists Terry Hunt and Carl Lipo systematically dismantled this ecocide mythology.</p>\n<p>Hunt and Lipo demonstrated through radiocarbon dating and paleoecological excavations that while deforestation did occur on Rapa Nui, it was largely driven by the invasive Polynesian rat (which devoured palm seeds) rather than wanton human destruction. Far more crucially, forest loss did not precipitate societal collapse. Instead, the islanders displayed extraordinary agronomic ingenuity: they adapted to deforestation by developing lithic mulch agriculture&mdash;deliberately blanketing volcanic soils with broken basalt rocks to conserve moisture, prevent wind erosion, and replenish essential soil nutrients. Far from falling into starvation and cannibalistic warfare, the Rapa Nui population remained stable, vibrant, and culturally productive for centuries after forest cover receded. The catastrophic demographic collapse of Rapa Nui occurred only after 1722, when European ships arrived, introducing deadly virgin-soil epidemics and carrying away more than a third of the surviving population into brutal Peruvian chattel slavery.</p>\n<p>The ideological hazard of Diamond's ecocide narrative, revisionist scholars emphasize, lies in its subtle erasure of colonial culpability. By framing the demographic demise of indigenous societies as self-inflicted ecological suicide, the ecocide paradigm performs an exculpatory historical laundering: European imperialists and slave raiders are absolved of guilt, transformed into mere witnesses to civilizations that had already destroyed themselves. Furthermore, treating human history as a binary choice between &ldquo;success&rdquo; and &ldquo;collapse&rdquo; obscures the pervasive reality of human resilience, institutional reorganization, and cultural adaptation. When environments change, human societies rarely undergo Hollywood-style instantaneous collapse; rather, they transform, migrate, adapt technologies, and renegotiate social orders. Environmental constraints are real, but human agency is not a passive casualty of ecological circumstance.</p>",
