    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 5 (Questions 25&ndash;30)</div>\n<p>For millennia, the historiography of Eurasia was dominated by the perspectives of sedentary, literate agrarian empires. From the dynastic chronicles of imperial China (such as Sima Qian's <i>Records of the Grand Historian</i>) to the treatises of Greco-Roman commentators, pastoral nomads of the Eurasian steppe&mdash;the Xiongnu, Turks, and Mongols&mdash;were almost uniformly depicted through a lens of civilizational terror. Nomadic pastoralists were characterized as uncivilized, predatory barbarians devoid of cities, laws, or culture, who emerged unpredictably from the barren northern wastes like a biblical scourge to pillage peaceful agrarian communities. In this Manichean framework, human history was viewed as a perpetual struggle between civilized agrarian order and anarchic nomadic chaos.</p>\n<p>Over the past four decades, however, revisionist global historians&mdash;such as Nicola Di Cosmo, Peter Golden, and Thomas Barfield&mdash;have dismantled this Sinocentric and Eurocentric orthodoxy. By synthesizing nomadic oral epics, archaeological excavations of steppe kurgans (burial mounds), and comparative economic anthropology, scholars have revealed pastoral nomads as sophisticated political architects, geopolitical integrators, and indispensable economic catalysts of transcontinental trade. Pastoral nomadism was not an impoverished, primitive precursor to agriculture, but a highly specialized, technologically advanced ecological adaptation to arid grasslands unsuitable for farming. Pastoralists domesticated horses, developed compound recurve bows, invented the stirrup, and mastered mounted archery, creating unprecedented mobility that allowed them to project military and administrative power across vast territories.</p>\n<p>Far from acting as parasitic destroyers of trade, nomadic confederations were the essential custodians and guarantors of the Silk Road. Nomads possessed an acute commercial consciousness. Because pastoral economies specialized in livestock and animal byproducts, they required manufactured goods, grains, tea, and luxury textiles from agrarian states. Rather than relying exclusively on raiding, nomadic empires actively forged symbiotic commercial relationships with sedentary neighbors. They constructed secure waystations (caravanserais), standardized tolls, suppressed banditry, and provided merchant caravans with pack camels, fresh horses, and armed military escorts. When the Mongol Empire consolidated political hegemony across Eurasia in the thirteenth century&mdash;establishing the famed <i>Pax Mongolica</i>&mdash;it instituted a transcontinental free-trade zone protected by the <i>yam</i> (an imperial postal and courier relay network) and the <i>paiza</i> (a diplomatic passport granting safe passage to merchants across thousands of miles).</p>\n<p>Moreover, nomadic empires developed ingenious dual-administration systems to govern diverse sedentary subjects without forcing pastoral cultural assimilation. In Khubilai Khan's Yuan dynasty in China or the Ilkhanate in Persia, nomadic rulers retained their martial steppe aristocracy while appointing Persian, Han, Tibetan, and Uighur scribes and administrators to oversee urban tax collection, legal administration, and irrigation maintenance. Under nomadic patronage, scientific and technological exchanges exploded: Chinese gunpowder, papermaking, and printing traversed westward to the Islamic world and Europe, while Persian astronomy, geometry, and Islamic medicine enriched Chinese imperial academies. The Silk Road was not a peripheral corridor carved out by sedentary merchants; it was an expansive trans-Eurasian communications highway engineered and maintained by steppe nomads.</p>",
