    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 6 (Questions 31&ndash;33)</div>\n<p>In July 1893, at a meeting of the American Historical Association held during the World's Columbian Exposition in Chicago, a young Wisconsin historian named Frederick Jackson Turner delivered an address that would define American historiography for half a century: &ldquo;The Significance of the Frontier in American History.&rdquo; Turner began with a striking observation from the Superintendent of the 1890 Census: that the continuous line of settlement that had long demarcated the American frontier had effectively ceased to exist. For Turner, this announcement signaled the close of the first great epoch in American history. Up to that point, Turner argued, the fundamental key to understanding American institutional development was not to be found in European &ldquo;germ theory&rdquo;&mdash;the conventional academic belief that American democracy was merely the transatlantic transplantation and unfolding of Anglo-Saxon Germanic tribal customs. Instead, Turner proclaimed that American democracy, egalitarianism, rugged individualism, and energetic nationalism were forged directly at the frontier: the outer edge of the wave, the meeting point between savagery and civilization.</p>\n<p>According to Turner's thesis, the wilderness masterfully transformed the European immigrant. Upon encountering the harsh North American wilderness, the European was stripped of established institutional baggage&mdash;hereditary social hierarchies, feudal land tenure, aristocratic deference, and scholastic dogmatism. To survive, the pioneer had to shed old European habits and adapt to the primitive demands of the wild. As wave after wave of settlers advanced westward from the Appalachian crest across the Mississippi Valley to the Pacific shore, this crucible of wilderness contact repeatedly rejuvenated the American character. It fostered an intense self-reliance, a restless practical energy, an impatience with formal restraint, and an instinctive egalitarianism that gave birth to authentic Jacksonian grassroots democracy. The frontier functioned as a perennial &ldquo;safety valve&rdquo; for urban discontent, siphoning off unemployed eastern laborers and preventing the emergence of rigid European-style proletarian class warfare.</p>\n<p>Yet Turner's lyrical frontier romance was founded on profound ideological erasures, which the &ldquo;New Western History&rdquo; school&mdash;pioneered in the late twentieth century by scholars like Patricia Nelson Limerick and Richard White&mdash;subjected to devastating critique. Limerick, in her seminal work <i>The Legacy of Conquest</i>, demonstrated that Turner's frontier was an ethnocentric myth that treated the American West as an &ldquo;empty, virgin wilderness&rdquo; awaiting heroic Anglo-Saxon settlement. In reality, the West was already an ancient, densely populated, and culturally diverse homeland inhabited by hundreds of sovereign Indigenous nations. What Turner romanticized as a peaceful advance of civilization across empty land was, in truth, a brutal, protracted process of imperial conquest, violent ethnic cleansing, forced reservation confinement, and cultural dispossession.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, New Western Historians exploded Turner's myth of agrarian rugged individualism by exposing the central role of the federal state and corporate capital in the development of the American West. The settlement of the West was not accomplished by lone homesteaders chopping trees with axes in heroic self-sufficiency. Rather, it was heavily subsidized, directed, and secured by the federal government: the U.S. Army dispossessed Native tribes; the federal treasury handed over millions of acres in land grants to transcontinental railroad conglomerates; and massive federal irrigation and dam projects re-engineered western river basins to sustain corporate agribusiness and resource extraction. Turner's safety valve was never a natural wilderness refuge, but an imperial zone thoroughly colonized by state power and global capital.</p>",
